---
import Icon from '@components/Icon.astro'
import PolicySection from '@components/PolicySection.astro'
import Page from '@layouts/Page.astro'
import {
  CONTAINER_PATH,
  formatDate,
  POLICY_UPDATED,
  PRIVACY_DESCRIPTION,
  PRIVACY_TITLE,
  SUPPORT_EMAIL,
  withBase,
} from '@utils/site'
---

<Page
  title={PRIVACY_TITLE}
  description={PRIVACY_DESCRIPTION}
  heading="Privacy Policy"
  updated={formatDate(POLICY_UPDATED)}
  current="privacy"
>
  <div class="border-line bg-surface rounded-card border p-6">
    <div class="grid grid-cols-[2.75rem_minmax(0,1fr)] items-start gap-x-3">
      <Icon name="shield-check" size={36} class="text-leaf-ink" />
      <div>
        <h2 class="mt-0 mb-2 text-xl font-semibold">The short version</h2>
        <p class="mb-0">
          Taskmato runs entirely on your Mac. It has no servers, accounts, or analytics. The app
          does not send your data anywhere, and its sandbox configuration blocks outgoing network
          connections.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <PolicySection number={1} icon="folder" title="What Taskmato stores on your Mac">
    <p>
      Taskmato stores your focus sessions, local tasks, app settings, and information about active
      and recent tasks. If you use the Obsidian provider, it also stores a bookmark to the vault
      folder you selected. This data stays in the app's sandbox container at
      <code class="inline-block max-w-full overflow-x-auto align-bottom whitespace-nowrap"
        >{CONTAINER_PATH}</code
      >.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={2} icon="key" title="Optional access">
    <p>
      Taskmato does not request permissions when it launches. It asks for access only when you
      enable a feature that needs it. The built-in Local provider does not require additional
      access.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Apple Reminders</strong>: When you enable the Reminders provider, Taskmato requests
        full Reminders access. It can read, create, update, complete, reopen, and delete reminders.
        Taskmato may store a reminder's identifier and task details when it is active, recent, or
        linked to a focus session.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Obsidian vault</strong>: When you set up the Obsidian provider, you select a vault
        folder. Taskmato can read and update Markdown task files in that folder. It stores a
        security-scoped bookmark so it can open the same folder later.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Notifications</strong>: When you turn on phase-end alerts, Taskmato requests
        notification permission. It schedules notifications locally through macOS. Taskmato does not
        use push notifications or send notification content over a network.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can change Reminders and notification permissions in System Settings. To change the folder
      Taskmato can access, select a different Obsidian vault.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={3} icon="eye-off" title="What it does not do">
    <p>Taskmato does not have any of the following:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Network access, incoming or outgoing</li>
      <li>Analytics, telemetry, or crash-reporting SDKs</li>
      <li>Ads</li>
      <li>Accounts</li>
      <li>Third-party SDKs of any kind</li>
      <li>Access to your camera, microphone, contacts, location, Bluetooth, or USB devices</li>
    </ul>
    <p>You can inspect the installed app's sandbox entitlements with:</p>
    <pre
      class="bg-ink text-canvas rounded-card overflow-x-auto px-4 py-3"><code>codesign -dvvv --entitlements :- /Applications/Taskmato.app</code></pre>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={4} icon="alert-triangle" title="Crash reports">
    <p>
      If you install Taskmato from the Mac App Store and choose to share analytics with developers,
      Apple may provide crash and diagnostic information through App Store Connect. Apple controls
      this sharing through your macOS settings. The Taskmato app does not collect or transmit crash
      reports itself.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={5} icon="trash" title="Deleting your data">
    <p>
      Move Taskmato to the Trash, then delete its container directory at
      <code class="inline-block max-w-full overflow-x-auto align-bottom whitespace-nowrap"
        >{CONTAINER_PATH}</code
      > to remove the data Taskmato stored. This does not remove your Reminders or Obsidian content. Changes
      Taskmato already made there, such as completed reminders or edited task lines, remain.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={6} icon="doc-text" title="Future changes">
    <p>
      A paid Taskmato Pro tier, iCloud sync, and cloud-backed providers may be added in the future.
      They are not part of the current build. This policy will be updated before a feature changes
      how Taskmato handles your data.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>

  <PolicySection number={7} icon="question-circle" title="Contact">
    <p>
      Questions about this policy? Email <a href={`mailto:${SUPPORT_EMAIL}`}>{SUPPORT_EMAIL}</a>.
      See the <a href={withBase('/support')}>support page</a> for other support options.
    </p>
  </PolicySection>
</Page>
